# REQ-007: Search, Filter, Sort, Tags & Smart Views

**Priority:** Must Have
**Phase:** Documents Management Core (30-day delivery)
**Effort:** Medium
**Risk:** Medium
**Depends On:** REQ-001 (Unified Inbound Document Inbox), REQ-004 (Document Viewer), REQ-005 (File Format Support)

---

## Design Decision: Tags + Smart Views (not custom folders)

**Decision date:** 2026-05-20
**Rationale:** Custom user-created folders (DocMan model) were evaluated and rejected. Moving a document into a custom folder removes it from its canonical workflow state (Inbox → Filing → Coding), making it impossible to confirm whether it has been actioned. This creates a clinical safety risk: documents can sit in a folder indefinitely with no system-level visibility that they remain unprocessed.

**Adopted model:** Tags + system-generated Smart Views.
- **Tags** are metadata labels applied to documents. A tagged document remains in its workflow queue. Tags are filterable and enable workload division without disrupting the pipeline.
- **Smart Views** are system-generated views derived from tags and usage. Users do not manually create Smart Views. Tag-based views appear in the sidebar as folder-like entries and remain live queries, not containers.
- **Drag-to-Smart-View gesture** applies a tag to the dragged document (preserving workflow state). This retains the familiar drag-and-drop interaction from DocMan without its safety drawbacks.
- **Role-based tag creation:** all staff can apply tags; only practice admin users can create or remove tags from the practice taxonomy. This prevents tag proliferation.

---

## User Story

As a practice administrator or clinician,
I need to search, filter, sort, and organise documents using tags and Smart Views,
So that I can quickly locate any document, divide workload across my team, and manage documents efficiently — without removing them from the workflow pipeline.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] Users can search documents by metadata fields (patient name, document type, source, department, date, urgency). *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Users can filter the document list by any combination of metadata fields. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] When a search query or filter is applied, the document count badge on each workflow tab (Unmatched, Filing, Coding, Tasks) updates immediately to reflect the number of documents visible after filtering. The badge must show the filtered count and the total count side-by-side (e.g. **7 / 9**) so users can confirm that filtering has not permanently removed documents from the queue. When the search or filter is cleared, the badge reverts to the full count. *(Must Have — CLIN-010: empty state must never mask the full dataset)* 
- [ ] Users can sort the document list by any metadata field (ascending and descending). *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Moving the mouse over a document row in a Smart View highlights that row visually to indicate hover state. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Practice admin users can create, rename, and delete tags for the practice tag taxonomy. All practice staff can apply existing tags to documents. Non-admin users cannot create, rename, or delete tags — this restriction is enforced by the system and prevents uncontrolled tag proliferation. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Tag names must be unique within an organisation taxonomy (case-insensitive, whitespace-normalised). Duplicate tags are rejected. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] The tag list supports search and sort (A-Z and Z-A) to help users locate tags quickly in large taxonomies. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Before a tag is deleted, the system must display a confirmation warning to the practice admin that states: (a) the tag will be permanently removed from the taxonomy, (b) the tag will be removed from every document it is currently applied to, and (c) any Smart View whose filter criteria include this tag as their primary tag will be removed from all users' sidebars. The admin must explicitly confirm deletion; dismissing the warning cancels the action. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] On confirmed tag deletion: the tag is removed from all documents it was applied to (cascade); all Smart Views whose primary tag matches the deleted tag are deleted for all users in the organisation. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] A starter tag set is provided at practice setup covering common NHS document categories (e.g. GP Letter, Discharge Summary, Outpatient Letter, Pathology, Radiology, Referral, Safeguarding). Practices can adopt, rename, or remove entries from the starter set. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Any user can apply one or more tags to a document inline from the document list (right-click context menu, tag chip on the row, or keyboard shortcut). *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Applying a tag does not change the document's workflow state (Inbox / Filing Queue / Coding Queue). Tags are additive metadata only. *(Must Have — clinical safety)*
- [ ] Users do not manually create Smart Views. Smart Views are generated by the system from tags and document usage patterns, and appear in the sidebar as folder-like entries. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Dragging a document to a Smart View in the sidebar applies the Smart View's primary tag to the document. The document's workflow queue is not changed. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] The tag taxonomy is organisation-scoped: each organisation has its own independent set of tags. Tags from one organisation are never visible to, or applicable within, any other organisation. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Practice admin users can configure the maximum number of tags allowed for their organisation. The system default is 50. The configurable range is 1–200. A warning is shown to practice admins when the organisation reaches 80% of its configured maximum. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] Tags unused for 90 consecutive days are flagged to practice admins for review. *(Should Have)*

**Acceptance Criteria — Bulk Actions within Smart Views:**
- [ ] Within a Smart View, users can select multiple documents using checkboxes, including a select-all control for the current view. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] A **Bulk Code** action is available when one or more documents are selected. The action allows the user to choose one or more SNOMED codes once and apply them to the patient record corresponding to each selected document. A document is eligible for bulk coding if it is confirmed patient-matched. *(Must Have)*
- [ ] If a selection contains documents that are ineligible for bulk coding (not patient-matched), those documents are clearly identified and excluded before the confirmation step; the action proceeds only for eligible documents. *(Must Have — clinical safety)*
- [ ] Before a bulk code action is executed, a mandatory confirmation step is presented that cannot be bypassed, showing: (a) the count of eligible documents that will be actioned, (b) a list of the patient names/identifiers whose records will be updated, and (c) the exact SNOMED code(s) to be applied to each patient record. *(Must Have — clinical safety)*
- [ ] After a bulk code action completes, the per-document outcome is reported: each document is marked as succeeded or failed with the specific error reason shown for failures. Successfully actioned documents are not rolled back if other documents in the batch fail. *(Must Have — clinical safety)*
- [ ] All bulk code events are individually audited: each patient record coding event generates a separate audit entry per REQ-009, recording user ERN, document ERN, patient ERN, and timestamp. *(Must Have)*

